The Long Game =C2=B7 Strategy weekly =C2=B7 Issue 85

THE ARCTIC IS A SHIPPING STORY, NOT A MILITARY ONE (YET)

Every summer brings a new round of Arctic alarm: bases reopened, icebreakers =
launched, flags planted. Most of it misreads the region.

The Northern Sea Route carried a record tonnage last season, but that still a=
mounts to a rounding error next to the Suez Canal. Insurance is expensive, th=
e season is short, and the route depends on Russian escorts and Russian permi=
ssion. For shippers it is an option, not a highway.

What is new is who is buying the option. Chinese carriers now make up most of=
 the non-Russian transits, and Beijing's "Polar Silk Road" language has quiet=
ly turned into ice-class hulls and port investment. Russia needs the cargo an=
d the capital; China wants a route that bypasses the Malacca Strait. Their in=
terests overlap, but they are not identical. Moscow is wary of anyone else ga=
ining standing in waters it considers its own.

For the West the useful questions are practical. Who builds icebreakers (the =
US has two working heavy ones)? Who maps the seabed? Who insures the ships? S=
trategic competition in the Arctic will be decided by these boring capacities=
 long before it is decided by deployments.

The military story will come. The shipping story is already here.

Reading this week
- A shipbuilding analyst on why icebreakers take a decade
- Satellite imagery of new Arctic port works, annotated

